1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,076
Here is a hospital database,
one row per patient.

2
00:00:03,396 --> 00:00:08,668
A researcher asks an innocent question:
how many patients have condition X?

3
00:00:08,989 --> 00:00:10,687
The hospital releases no records.

4
00:00:11,007 --> 00:00:13,076
It just says: 41.

5
00:00:13,317 --> 00:00:16,291
A week later,
one new patient, Alice, is admitted.

6
00:00:16,611 --> 00:00:18,211
The researcher asks again.

7
00:00:18,423 --> 00:00:20,023
The answer is now 42.

8
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:24,547
Subtract, and the researcher has learned
something very specific about Alice,

9
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:26,310
without seeing a single record.

10
00:00:26,630 --> 00:00:29,697
Before we go on, try to fix this yourself.

11
00:00:30,017 --> 00:00:34,459
Pause and ponder: would rounding the count
to the nearest ten protect Alice?

12
00:00:34,779 --> 00:00:37,954
Pause the video if you need more time.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,527
Most weeks, yes.

14
00:00:50,847 --> 00:00:53,407
But if the count goes from 44 to 45,

15
00:00:53,407 --> 00:00:58,171
the rounded answer jumps from 40 to 50,
and Alice is exposed again.

16
00:00:58,491 --> 00:01:03,659
Any fixed rule that ever changes its answer
has a jump like that somewhere.

17
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:07,524
So what would it even mean
for a statistic to be private?

18
00:01:07,844 --> 00:01:10,511
And if the fix is to add noise,
how much is enough?

19
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:16,033
In 2006, Cynthia Dwork, Frank McSherry,
Kobbi Nissim and Adam Smith

20
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:19,030
answered both questions in this paper:

21
00:01:19,030 --> 00:01:23,603
Calibrating Noise to Sensitivity
in Private Data Analysis.

22
00:01:23,923 --> 00:01:28,209
It founded
what we now call differential privacy,

23
00:01:28,209 --> 00:01:31,270
and in 2017 it won the Gödel Prize.

24
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:36,426
This video covers its three big ideas:
a definition of privacy,

25
00:01:36,426 --> 00:01:41,958
a number called sensitivity,
and a recipe for how much noise is enough.

26
00:01:42,278 --> 00:01:46,157
Plus a surprising limit
on what one published table can achieve,

27
00:01:46,157 --> 00:01:48,339
if it must be private in this sense.

28
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:53,262
First, a map of where this paper comes from.

29
00:01:53,582 --> 00:01:58,634
In 1965, Stanley Warner proposed
randomized response for sensitive surveys.

30
00:01:58,954 --> 00:02:01,919
In a popular version,
you flip a coin in private.

31
00:02:02,239 --> 00:02:04,154
Heads, you answer truthfully.

32
00:02:04,474 --> 00:02:08,523
Tails, you flip again and say yes for heads,
no for tails.

33
00:02:08,843 --> 00:02:11,581
Any single yes could be the coin talking,

34
00:02:11,581 --> 00:02:15,988
yet over thousands of people
the true rate can still be estimated.

35
00:02:16,308 --> 00:02:17,666
Keep this coin in mind.

36
00:02:17,716 --> 00:02:19,395
Over the following decades,

37
00:02:19,395 --> 00:02:23,001
statisticians and computer scientists
refined such tricks,

38
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:27,917
in two flavours: scramble the data going in,
or scramble the answers coming out.

39
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:33,670
Meanwhile, the obvious fix,
just remove the names, kept failing.

40
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:35,520
Latanya Sweeney showed

41
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:40,111
that ZIP code, birth date and sex alone
single out most Americans,

42
00:02:40,111 --> 00:02:44,237
and in 1997 she linked
supposedly anonymous hospital records

43
00:02:44,237 --> 00:02:48,569
to a public voter list
and found the governor of Massachusetts.

44
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:52,901
The lesson:
privacy must be a property of the process,

45
00:02:52,901 --> 00:02:55,607
not of how the released table looks,

46
00:02:55,607 --> 00:02:59,365
and it must hold
whatever else the attacker knows.

47
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:05,714
Then, in 2003, Irit Dinur and Kobbi Nissim
proved something sobering.

48
00:03:06,034 --> 00:03:08,022
Answer too many questions too accurately,

49
00:03:08,022 --> 00:03:10,495
with errors much smaller
than the square root of n,

50
00:03:10,495 --> 00:03:13,162
and an attacker can rebuild
almost the entire database.

51
00:03:13,482 --> 00:03:18,821
Our subtraction
was the two-question version of this attack.

52
00:03:19,141 --> 00:03:21,907
Noise is the price of answering many questions.

53
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:23,392
The hopeful flip side:

54
00:03:23,392 --> 00:03:26,721
with a limited number of questions,
modest noise is enough.

55
00:03:27,041 --> 00:03:32,562
Dwork, Nissim and colleagues built this
into a framework called SuLQ,

56
00:03:32,562 --> 00:03:37,442
for sub-linear queries,
which answers noisy sums like counts.

57
00:03:37,762 --> 00:03:39,362
Remember that limit on questions.

58
00:03:39,497 --> 00:03:41,959
This paper turns it into a budget.

59
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,201
But SuLQ only covered sums,

60
00:03:44,201 --> 00:03:48,574
and its definition tolerated
a tiny chance of a large leak.

61
00:03:48,894 --> 00:03:52,781
This paper takes the leap:
any function of the data,

62
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:56,967
one clean definition,
and one simple rule for the noise.

63
00:03:57,287 --> 00:04:00,443
Fittingly,
it appeared at a cryptography conference,

64
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:05,056
where the habit is to define security
against every possible attacker first,

65
00:04:05,056 --> 00:04:06,656
and then prove it.

66
00:04:08,583 --> 00:04:10,277
Here is the setting.

67
00:04:10,597 --> 00:04:17,340
A trusted server, the curator,
holds a database x: n rows, one per person.

68
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:19,252
An analyst sends a query f,

69
00:04:19,252 --> 00:04:23,555
any function of the database
that returns a number, or a list of numbers.

70
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,388
The curator never releases
the honest answer, f(x).

71
00:04:27,708 --> 00:04:32,659
Instead it draws random noise, Y,
and releases f(x) + Y.

72
00:04:32,979 --> 00:04:37,677
Because the analyst can keep asking,
this is called the interactive setting.

73
00:04:37,997 --> 00:04:44,038
The alternative, publishing one sanitized table
and walking away, is non-interactive.

74
00:04:44,358 --> 00:04:47,838
At the end, the paper proves
the first is fundamentally more powerful.

75
00:04:48,083 --> 00:04:52,716
So everything hinges on one question:
what distribution should Y have?

76
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:55,528
Too little noise, and Alice is exposed.

77
00:04:55,848 --> 00:04:57,629
Too much, and the answer is useless.

78
00:04:57,949 --> 00:05:01,676
The title gives the answer:
calibrate the noise to the sensitivity.

79
00:05:01,996 --> 00:05:05,750
But first, what does private actually mean?

80
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:08,755
The key move is to imagine two worlds.

81
00:05:09,075 --> 00:05:10,924
In one, the database is x.

82
00:05:11,244 --> 00:05:14,715
In the other, it is x′:
identical except for one row.

83
00:05:15,035 --> 00:05:16,650
Say, Alice's.

84
00:05:16,970 --> 00:05:19,199
Databases like this are called neighbors.

85
00:05:19,519 --> 00:05:23,390
In the hospital, Alice was added;
here, her row just says something different.

86
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:26,490
Either way,
no single person's row should matter much.

87
00:05:26,733 --> 00:05:28,109
Run the mechanism,

88
00:05:28,109 --> 00:05:32,314
the curator's randomized answering rule,
in each world.

89
00:05:32,634 --> 00:05:36,847
Whatever the analyst gets to see,
the paper calls the transcript;

90
00:05:36,847 --> 00:05:38,856
for now, a single noisy answer.

91
00:05:39,176 --> 00:05:40,763
Because the mechanism is random,

92
00:05:40,763 --> 00:05:43,639
each world gives a whole distribution
of possible outputs.

93
00:05:43,883 --> 00:05:47,631
Pick any output t,
and compare the heights of the two curves there.

94
00:05:47,951 --> 00:05:50,083
Slide t along and watch their ratio.

95
00:05:50,403 --> 00:05:55,835
A private mechanism keeps
that ratio close to 1 everywhere:

96
00:05:55,835 --> 00:05:59,149
never above e^ε, never below e^(−ε).

97
00:05:59,383 --> 00:06:03,134
That is the paper's definition,
which it calls ε-indistinguishability.

98
00:06:03,454 --> 00:06:06,855
The log of the ratio is the privacy loss at t.

99
00:06:07,175 --> 00:06:11,482
For every pair of neighbors,
every analyst and every output,

100
00:06:11,482 --> 00:06:14,282
it must be at most ε in absolute value.

101
00:06:14,602 --> 00:06:19,452
The absolute value makes it symmetric:
a ratio of 2 and a ratio of 1/2 count the same.

102
00:06:19,772 --> 00:06:25,363
For small ε, e^ε is about 1 + ε.

103
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,914
Let's test it on Warner's coin from our map.

104
00:06:28,234 --> 00:06:35,179
Pause and ponder: if Alice's true answer is yes,
how likely is she to say yes? And if it is no?

105
00:06:48,017 --> 00:06:51,964
If the truth is yes,
she says yes 3/4 of the time:

106
00:06:51,964 --> 00:06:54,096
heads, or tails then heads.

107
00:06:54,416 --> 00:06:59,017
If it is no,
only the second coin can say yes: 1/4.

108
00:06:59,337 --> 00:07:04,426
The worst ratio is 3,
so the coin satisfies the definition,

109
00:07:04,426 --> 00:07:06,582
with ε = ln 3, about 1.1.

110
00:07:06,817 --> 00:07:09,908
Now be the attacker,
trying to tell the two worlds apart.

111
00:07:10,228 --> 00:07:16,238
By Bayes' rule, your new odds
are your old odds times exactly this ratio,

112
00:07:16,238 --> 00:07:21,672
so they move by at most a factor of e^ε,
whatever you knew before.

113
00:07:21,992 --> 00:07:29,098
Starting from 50/50,
ε = 0.1 leaves you at most 52.5% sure;

114
00:07:29,098 --> 00:07:31,386
ε = 1, at most 73%.

115
00:07:31,706 --> 00:07:35,564
ε is set by policy;
the paper calls it the leakage.

116
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:37,465
Notice what this does not promise.

117
00:07:37,785 --> 00:07:41,341
If a study reveals
that smokers get more heart disease,

118
00:07:41,341 --> 00:07:45,284
and Alice smokes,
people may now think she is at higher risk.

119
00:07:45,604 --> 00:07:47,446
That is not a privacy violation:

120
00:07:47,446 --> 00:07:50,843
the same lesson could be learned
from everyone else's data,

121
00:07:50,843 --> 00:07:54,887
so it would happen even if Alice's row
were replaced by someone else's.

122
00:07:56,333 --> 00:07:59,099
Why demand a ratio bound
for every single output?

123
00:07:59,419 --> 00:08:02,217
Cryptography often settles for something weaker:

124
00:08:02,217 --> 00:08:05,655
statistical distance,
half the area between the two curves.

125
00:08:05,975 --> 00:08:10,470
Equivalently, the most the probability
of any event can differ between the worlds.

126
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:13,238
The paper shows why that is not enough here.

127
00:08:13,483 --> 00:08:17,122
Consider this mechanism: pick one row at random
and publish it, word for word.

128
00:08:17,442 --> 00:08:21,719
Change one person's row,
and the output distribution moves by only 1/n.

129
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:29,580
For a big database that is tiny,
so by the averaged measure this looks private.

130
00:08:29,816 --> 00:08:32,666
But every output is somebody's complete record.

131
00:08:32,986 --> 00:08:34,798
The ratio test catches it:

132
00:08:34,798 --> 00:08:39,745
the output showing Alice's real value
has probability 1/n in one world,

133
00:08:39,745 --> 00:08:41,339
and 0 in the other.

134
00:08:41,389 --> 00:08:42,900
The ratio is infinite.

135
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:47,365
Averages let catastrophes slip through,
as long as each is rare for any single person.

136
00:08:47,685 --> 00:08:50,585
A ratio bound rules them out everywhere.

137
00:08:50,905 --> 00:08:53,786
It also settles our rounding question:
at the jump,

138
00:08:53,786 --> 00:08:58,135
one world gives that answer with probability 1,
the other with probability 0.

139
00:08:58,455 --> 00:09:01,547
To pass this test, a mechanism has to be random.

140
00:09:02,983 --> 00:09:04,701
So how much noise do we need?

141
00:09:05,021 --> 00:09:08,527
It depends on a single property of the query:
its sensitivity,

142
00:09:08,527 --> 00:09:11,806
the most that changing one row
can ever change the answer,

143
00:09:11,806 --> 00:09:16,215
over all pairs of neighboring databases,
including ones that do not exist yet.

144
00:09:16,450 --> 00:09:21,382
For a counting query, like our hospital count,
one row changes the answer by at most 1.

145
00:09:21,702 --> 00:09:23,000
Sensitivity 1.

146
00:09:23,050 --> 00:09:26,737
Now a histogram:
split the possible values of a row into d bins,

147
00:09:26,737 --> 00:09:28,984
and release how many rows fall in each.

148
00:09:29,304 --> 00:09:32,885
Before I show you, pause:
if Alice's row changes,

149
00:09:32,885 --> 00:09:36,466
how much can the whole histogram
change in total?

150
00:09:36,786 --> 00:09:38,447
Does it depend on the number of bins?

151
00:09:47,283 --> 00:09:52,133
She moves out of one bin and into another:
one count goes down by 1, another goes up by 1.

152
00:09:52,453 --> 00:09:54,053
Total change: 2.

153
00:09:54,260 --> 00:09:58,654
Adding up absolute changes
across coordinates like this is the L1 norm,

154
00:09:58,654 --> 00:10:02,366
the paper's way to measure sensitivity
for lists of numbers.

155
00:10:02,686 --> 00:10:06,646
And it is 2 whether there are 5 bins or 5000.

156
00:10:06,966 --> 00:10:08,620
It does not depend on the dimension at all.

157
00:10:08,866 --> 00:10:12,028
Contrast that with:
what is the largest income in the database?

158
00:10:12,348 --> 00:10:17,523
One billionaire can move that answer by $1B,
and with no cap there is no limit at all.

159
00:10:17,843 --> 00:10:21,620
The standard fix is to cap every value,
say at $1M, first.

160
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:23,201
Remember that trick:

161
00:10:23,201 --> 00:10:26,918
it is exactly what private deep learning
does to gradients.

162
00:10:27,166 --> 00:10:28,570
Two things to remember.

163
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:31,136
Sensitivity is a property of the function alone,

164
00:10:31,136 --> 00:10:34,438
not of the particular database,
and it is not chosen by policy.

165
00:10:34,758 --> 00:10:36,518
ε is a choice.

166
00:10:36,838 --> 00:10:38,438
Sensitivity is a fact.

167
00:10:39,817 --> 00:10:41,394
Now for the noise.

168
00:10:41,444 --> 00:10:45,291
The paper uses the Laplace distribution:
symmetric, sharply peaked,

169
00:10:45,291 --> 00:10:48,736
and falling off exponentially
with distance from the center.

170
00:10:49,056 --> 00:10:51,969
Its width is set by a scale parameter, λ.

171
00:10:53,017 --> 00:10:54,617
Here is the trick.

172
00:10:54,689 --> 00:11:00,760
Center one Laplace curve at f(x), 41,
and another at f(x′), 42:

173
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:04,905
the output distributions of our two worlds.

174
00:11:05,150 --> 00:11:09,279
Now plot the log of their ratio
for every output t.

175
00:11:09,599 --> 00:11:12,343
To the left of 41, it is exactly constant.

176
00:11:12,663 --> 00:11:14,937
To the right of 42, constant again.

177
00:11:15,257 --> 00:11:17,220
In between, a straight ramp.

178
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:21,910
It never rises above 1/λ,
and never falls below −1/λ:

179
00:11:21,910 --> 00:11:24,549
the shift, divided by the scale.

180
00:11:25,184 --> 00:11:26,184
Why?

181
00:11:26,234 --> 00:11:33,276
On a log scale, a Laplace density is a tent,
whose sides have slope 1/λ, never steeper.

182
00:11:33,596 --> 00:11:34,783
Slide the tent over,

183
00:11:34,783 --> 00:11:39,177
and the gap between the tents
can never exceed the slide times that slope.

184
00:11:39,497 --> 00:11:41,702
That is the whole design principle:

185
00:11:41,702 --> 00:11:45,043
privacy needs noise
whose log density is never steep.

186
00:11:45,784 --> 00:11:51,157
In symbols: two distances to t can differ
by at most the distance between the centers.

187
00:11:51,477 --> 00:11:56,252
So the privacy loss of any output
is at most the sensitivity divided by λ.

188
00:11:56,572 --> 00:12:02,422
Set λ to the sensitivity over ε,
and the ratio stays within e^ε,

189
00:12:02,422 --> 00:12:06,535
for every output and every pair of neighbors.

190
00:12:06,855 --> 00:12:09,188
Privacy, proven in one line.

191
00:12:09,434 --> 00:12:14,998
If the query returns several numbers,
add independent Laplace noise to each.

192
00:12:15,318 --> 00:12:18,588
The joint density
then depends on the L1 distance,

193
00:12:18,588 --> 00:12:22,512
which is exactly why sensitivity
is measured in the L1 norm.

194
00:12:22,832 --> 00:12:24,918
That is Proposition 1:

195
00:12:24,918 --> 00:12:29,661
Laplace noise of scale S(f)/ε
in every coordinate.

196
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:36,324
Pause and ponder: why not something simpler,
like uniform noise anywhere between −10 and +10?

197
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:48,365
Look at the edges.

198
00:12:48,415 --> 00:12:50,774
An output of 51.5 is possible

199
00:12:50,774 --> 00:12:54,923
if the true count is 42
but impossible if it is 41:

200
00:12:54,923 --> 00:12:59,664
the same infinite ratio
that sank the random-row mechanism.

201
00:12:59,984 --> 00:13:03,383
Good noise must never rule an output out.

202
00:13:03,617 --> 00:13:06,116
And why not the familiar bell curve?

203
00:13:06,436 --> 00:13:10,633
On a log scale a Gaussian is a parabola,
steeper and steeper,

204
00:13:10,633 --> 00:13:14,830
so the gap between two shifted parabolas
grows without bound.

205
00:13:15,150 --> 00:13:19,154
In the tails the ratio explodes,
and no single ε works.

206
00:13:19,474 --> 00:13:22,178
Hold on to that Gaussian, though: it comes back,

207
00:13:22,178 --> 00:13:26,629
and it is the noise you would actually use
to train a neural network privately.

208
00:13:26,867 --> 00:13:33,486
So here is the recipe in the title:
noise scale equals sensitivity divided by ε.

209
00:13:33,806 --> 00:13:36,388
A more sensitive question needs more noise.

210
00:13:36,708 --> 00:13:38,820
Stronger privacy needs more noise.

211
00:13:40,367 --> 00:13:45,770
Back to the hospital, with ε = 0.5:
Laplace noise of scale 2.

212
00:13:46,090 --> 00:13:49,510
Week 1, the curator reports about 43.7.

213
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:54,031
Week 2, when the true count is 42, about 40.6.

214
00:13:54,351 --> 00:13:58,347
Subtract as before,
and Alice seems to have lowered the count by 3.

215
00:13:58,667 --> 00:14:04,166
A 50/50 guess about her can now move
to at most about 62%.

216
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:08,505
Now look at what the noise scale depends on:
the sensitivity and ε.

217
00:14:08,825 --> 00:14:10,511
Not the size of the database.

218
00:14:11,350 --> 00:14:16,088
With 100 patients, noise of typical size 2
is a few percent of the answer.

219
00:14:16,408 --> 00:14:19,678
With a million, it is a rounding error.

220
00:14:19,998 --> 00:14:24,288
Alice is protected by noise
that is large compared to her own contribution,

221
00:14:24,288 --> 00:14:27,149
which is 1,
but tiny compared to the population's.

222
00:14:28,384 --> 00:14:30,180
Privacy for individuals.

223
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,163
Accuracy for populations.

224
00:14:32,483 --> 00:14:34,405
That is the bargain.

225
00:14:34,650 --> 00:14:36,627
Here is one you can answer yourself.

226
00:14:36,947 --> 00:14:40,948
Pause and ponder: the noise has size about 1/ε.

227
00:14:41,268 --> 00:14:45,338
What goes wrong if ε is much smaller than 1/n?

228
00:14:58,184 --> 00:15:01,851
The noise outgrows n,
bigger than the count could ever be,

229
00:15:01,851 --> 00:15:03,685
and the answer is pure noise.

230
00:15:04,005 --> 00:15:06,129
This is not Laplace's fault.

231
00:15:06,449 --> 00:15:12,595
Any two databases are linked
by a chain of at most n single-row changes,

232
00:15:12,595 --> 00:15:19,509
each changing probabilities by at most e^ε,
so the ends differ by at most e^(nε).

233
00:15:19,829 --> 00:15:25,381
If nε is tiny, every database looks alike,
and nothing can be learned.

